# Files bash reads stay LF in every working copy, whatever core.autocrlf says: a Windows clone with autocrlf=true would
# otherwise check them out CRLF, and bash (Git Bash runs the UCI shell suite and the hooks on Windows) reads "\r" as
# part of each command.
*.sh        text eol=lf
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# Data the engine embeds (include_bytes!) or a test reads byte for byte is checked out exactly as committed, with no
# line-ending conversion: under autocrlf=true a Windows build would embed a CRLF book where Linux and macOS embed LF -
# a byte-different binary for the same commit. The C++ and Go engines keep theirs the same way.
pawnstar.book            -text
tests/nnue_reference.txt -text
